   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Dionisis Margaris</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Konstantinos I. Roumeliotis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Dimitris Spiliotopoulos and Costas Vassilakis</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Decision Models as Product Rerankers for E-Business Search and Recommendation: An Independent Evaluation on the Amazon Shopping Queries Dataset</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Decision Models as Product Rerankers for E-Business Search and Recommendation: An Independent Evaluation on the Amazon Shopping Queries Dataset</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>product search; e-commerce; reranking; decision models; large language models; Amazon Shopping Queries (ESCI); WANDS; nDCG; substitute and complementary products</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Retailers increasingly let language models decide which products shoppers see first. Decision models (Jev</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Retailers increasingly let language models decide which products shoppers see first. Decision models (Jev</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Clef and Perplexity Decider) return a probability for each permitted judgement</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Clef and Perplexity Decider) return a probability for each permitted judgement</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">not generated text</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">not generated text</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">which makes them natural rerankers. We present the first independent evaluation of decision models on the Amazon Shopping Queries (ESCI) benchmark: 1000 US-English</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">which makes them natural rerankers. We present the first independent evaluation of decision models on the Amazon Shopping Queries (ESCI) benchmark: 1000 US-English</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">200 Spanish and 200 Japanese queries. Comparisons included a dedicated reranker (Qwen3-Reranker)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">200 Spanish and 200 Japanese queries. Comparisons included a dedicated reranker (Qwen3-Reranker)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">general-purpose language models</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">general-purpose language models</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">BM25 and a supervised reference; two reranker sizes with three instructions each</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">BM25 and a supervised reference; two reranker sizes with three instructions each</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">a second BM25 analyser and three further settings were added after a pre-specified main run. Every decision model improved nDCG@10 over BM25 in every market</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">a second BM25 analyser and three further settings were added after a pre-specified main run. Every decision model improved nDCG@10 over BM25 in every market</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">by 0.083 to 0.172. Against the strongest reranker configuration</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">by 0.083 to 0.172. Against the strongest reranker configuration</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Perplexity Decider was significantly better in English (0.822 versus 0.795) and on a second retailer's benchmark (WANDS)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Perplexity Decider was significantly better in English (0.822 versus 0.795) and on a second retailer's benchmark (WANDS)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and when run locally</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and when run locally</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">in Japanese as well; most others were statistically tied. Behind a first-stage retriever</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">in Japanese as well; most others were statistically tied. Behind a first-stage retriever</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">gains over BM25 persisted</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">gains over BM25 persisted</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but the strongest reranker matched or beat every decision model. A training-split probe found no sign that zero-shot systems had learned from the ESCI training split. Question design (four-way</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but the strongest reranker matched or beat every decision model. A training-split probe found no sign that zero-shot systems had learned from the ESCI training split. Question design (four-way</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">ordinal or yes/no) mattered little. All systems confused Exact with Substitute products</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">ordinal or yes/no) mattered little. All systems confused Exact with Substitute products</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and Complement recall in English ranged from 15 to 68. The hosted Perplexity API reranked 1000 queries for US0.10--0.14</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and Complement recall in English ranged from 15 to 68. The hosted Perplexity API reranked 1000 queries for US0.10--0.14</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but its outputs changed within a month.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but its outputs changed within a month.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
